# Phronesis Frontend Foundation Walkthrough

The initial setup for the Phronesis Homeschool frontend (`phronesis-web`) is complete! We have established an institutional-grade, scalable foundation that perfectly aligns with your backend domain-driven architecture.

## What Was Accomplished

### 1. Framework & Core Technologies
- Scaffolding of the frontend using **Next.js (App Router)**.
- Integrated **Tailwind CSS** for rapid and modern UI development.
- Configured **TypeScript** in strict mode to ensure robust type safety and prevent runtime errors.

### 2. State Management & Data Fetching
- Installed and configured **Zustand** (for simple, unopinionated global state) and **Tanstack React Query** (for data fetching, caching, and server state synchronization).
- Created a `QueryProvider` and integrated it at the Root Layout level to make React Query available throughout the app.
- Configured a base **Axios API client** (`src/lib/api.ts`) equipped with standard request/response interceptors to easily plug into your ASP.NET Core backend (`Phronesis.Api`), specifically configured to attach authentication tokens automatically.

### 3. Feature-Driven Architecture
- Structured the `src/` directory to mirror the domain boundaries of the Phronesis platform. We now have dedicated folders inside `src/features/` for:
  - `identity`
  - `academic`
  - `content`
  - `learning`
  - `tuition`
  - `commerce`
- These folders are primed to hold their own specific components, hooks, stores, types, and API logic, promoting highly decoupled and maintainable code.
- Added standard shared directories such as `components`, `hooks`, `lib`, `types`, and `utils`.

## Validation

- **Dependencies Verified**: All required dependencies (`zod`, `react-hook-form`, `axios`, etc.) were installed successfully.
- **Production Build Passed**: We ran `npm run build`, which compiled successfully without any TypeScript, routing, or ESLint errors. The static generation and route rendering passed with a perfect exit code.

> [!TIP]
> **Next Steps**
> You can now dive into building out the UI components and pages! We recommend starting with the `identity` feature to build the authentication flow, hooking it up using the configured Axios client and React Query. You can start the local development server at any time by running `npm run dev`.
